title: Field aspects
tags: Query
kind: reference
aliases: aspect aspects
summary: field:value, field:parent and field:path say which part of a value a predicate reads; on a tree_ref, = is the exact node.

A field reference may name an ''aspect'': which component of the value the predicate
reads. The DSL spells it `field:aspect`; the IR carries an `aspect` key, omitted for
`raw`. The same four names serve the GUI's columns and finder, so one vocabulary
describes reading a value everywhere.

|!Aspect |!Reads |!Applies to |
|`raw` |the value itself — on a `tree_ref`, the node |every type (the default) |
|`value` |the row's own text — on a `tree_ref`, the node's own name |every type |
|`parent` |a `tree_ref`'s parent node |`tree_ref` only |
|`path` |the whole path from the root, `a/b/c` |`tree_ref` only |

`parent` or `path` on a field that is not a `tree_ref` is refused with `400`, and so
is an aspect on `IS UNKNOWN`.

''Equality is the exact node.'' On a `tree_ref`, `=`/`!=` with a string is resolved
to the one node at that path ([[TreeRef path conventions]]): `mfr_path =
"/music/jazz"`, `path = "music/jazz"`, and a forest root by its bare name,
`path = "music"`. A path that is no node matches nothing. It costs one lookup, and so
does `!=` — which is not the complement of `=`: a metarecord without the field
satisfies neither. By leaf name wherever it sits, it is `mfr_path:value = "2021"`. On
any other type `raw` and `value` coincide: `title = "x"` is `title:value = "x"`.

''An aspect is required where `raw` means nothing.'' Ordering or regex-matching a
`(parent, name)` couple has no meaning, so on a `tree_ref` the ordered operators and
`MATCHES` are refused with `400` unless `:value` or `:path` is given — `path:value <
"m"`, `path:path MATCHES "^music/"`. Silently reading the name instead would be the
kind of implicit rule aspects exist to remove.

''Forest roots.'' `field:parent IS ABSENT` matches the nodes whose parent is absent:
the roots of the forest — the only predicate form for them, since `->` cannot name
"no parent" ([[Tree endpoints]] has `tree/roots`). `field:parent IS PRESENT` is every
other node. `field:parent = "<path>"` is the same set as `field -> "<path>"`, spelled
as a comparison; `field:parent != "<path>"` is every node under another parent, roots
included, and every node when the path is no node. All four are bitmap lookups, not
scans.

''`parent` reads a node'', so only the equality-shaped predicates apply to it:
`IS PRESENT`, `IS ABSENT`, `=`, `!=`. An ordered operator or `MATCHES` under `parent`
is refused, naming `:value` and `:path` as the candidates.

''Sorting is not aspect-aware'': a `tree_ref` sort always orders by the whole path
([[Pagination and sorting]]) — selecting by name is useful, ordering by name alone
just interleaves every folder.

Why the vocabulary exists: `path = "…"` once meant both "this node" and "this name",
depending on a `/` in the operand, and a forest root could not be addressed at all.
The idiom "parent plus name" (`f -> "/parent" AND f = "name"`) broke silently when
`=` changed meaning; search for it before touching the semantics of `=`.
